# Crate-local, because this directory is its OWN cargo workspace root
# (see the `[workspace]` table in Cargo.toml) and so gets its own build
# artefacts and its own lockfile, neither of which the repository root's
# .gitignore anticipates.

# Build artefacts.
target/

# This crate's lockfile. It is a library, it is `publish = false`, and the
# thing whose dependency versions actually need pinning is the application
# that consumes it — `examples/tauri-checkout/src-tauri` (Lane D), which
# carries its own lock. Committing one here would only give `cargo` a second
# resolution to keep in step with that one.
/Cargo.lock

# Written by `tauri_plugin::Builder` when building for an android/ios target:
# it copies Tauri's own mobile API project into `./.tauri/` (for the Swift
# package) and `android/.tauri/` (for the Gradle project) so the native
# sources can compile against it. Generated, large, and version-locked to
# whichever `tauri` the build resolved. `tauri-apps/plugins-workspace`
# ignores the same trees (`/plugins/*/.tauri`).
.tauri/

# NOT ignored, on purpose: `permissions/autogenerated/` and
# `permissions/schemas/`. `tauri_plugin::Builder` writes both from
# `build.rs`'s COMMANDS list, and Tauri's own plugins track them
# (`plugins/opener/permissions/{autogenerated,schemas}` in
# tauri-apps/plugins-workspace). Tracking them is what lets a consuming
# application resolve `vpay-checkout:allow-show` from a plain checkout — and
# what makes a change to the command list show up in a diff instead of only
# in someone's `target/`.
